I am really grateful to be back here as  Ian said I was University associate  university chaplain here for 12 years  and an intern before that so this Chapel  has been my spiritual home probably of  more than any place in my life I was  ordained here in this space let me think  I can't do the math but october twenty  four 1993 so it's been a while both our  boys spent their earliest years here  Connor who is now ten was baby Jesus  here his first Christmas I have  participated in memorial services and  weddings and baptisms and more sunday  mornings than I can count I am grateful  to Ian for inviting me and Sharon and  Calista and others in the chaplains  office for welcoming me back so warmly  on friday and to you all many of whom  are old friends and many I have not met  as the community has continued to grow  but on behalf of myself and my partner  mimi and our boys we are so glad to be  here I feel at home here in this space  like no other even though it is no  longer my everyday home it is the home  of my heart and of shared history and of  the current welcome and community so I  thank you and now let us pray may the  words of my mouth and the meditations of  all our hearts be acceptable in your  sight o God our rock and our Redeemer  amen one of my most precious memories  from this space is from sep tember of  1997 madeleine l'engle a beloved  connecticut author she wrote a wrinkle  in time for those of you who know that  book or and may or may not recognize her  name she spoke hear that sep tember she  was I think in her mid 70s at that time  vital and energetic with piercing eyes  but also needing to sit for her talk so  we brought up a wing chair from the  Levitt room and plopped her down in the  chair right there  I glimpsed what it might have been like  to be one of Jesus disciples to want to  sit at the feet of a teacher and just  listen and learn Madeleine spoke and  then she answered questions well she  kind of answered questions somewhat like  Jesus she used the questions to go where  she wanted to go never directly hitting  the question but giving us so much more  than we could have possibly thought to  ask kind of like Jesus that way what I  remember most from that evening was a  story that she shared a rabbi story she  called it rabbi said the student why in  Deuteronomy does it say we should write  the words of God on our hearts rather  than in our hearts replied the rabbi we  are not ready to have the words of God  in our hearts but if we write them on  our hearts when our hearts break they  will fall in mammal and linkle shared  that story on a Thursday evening and on  Friday morning Mimi flew to Tennessee to  be with her mother in her last days I  stayed through Sunday when I preached on  this rabbi story and then joined her her  mother died about 10 days later and  Mimi's heart broke as well as her  sisters and her dad and the words of God  in Scripture and song and poetry did  indeed fall into the broken hearts that  story has stayed with us and in all  these years many beloved words of God  continue to be written on our hearts and  in the hearts of our families and  friends they are written waiting to fall  in again when we need them  today's Gospel though doesn't seem to be  one of those stories that I would write  on my heart to comfort me in brokenness  it's not one of the scriptures that a  preacher longs to be assigned either Ian  sent me these texts long ago and I  looked quickly and thought yeah that's  good that's fine I can do the lectionary  but lo and behold it is not on the top  of my list to preach about the Sadducees  but there it is waiting for us waiting  for me this morning and I haven't been  able to avoid it so let's look at it  again a little bit this is the story of  the Sadducees trying to trick Jesus  testing his theology his understanding  of Torah his belief and his wits the  Sadducees don't believe in resurrection  so they come up with an extreme  situation to point out just how  ludicrous the idea of Resurrection is  and they're tricky they pick a situation  where to obey Jewish laws makes the idea  of Resurrection seem crazy a man marries  a woman and then he dies without having  children so according to Moses in the  Torah if a man dies leaving his widow  childless that man's brother must marry  her to provide children as through his  brother but the Sadducees aren't content  with just two brothers in this story two  marriages no no no they create a  scenario of seven brothers and that poor  woman who doesn't even seem to exist in  this story that's an aside though so in  the story all the brothers die Jesus who  will she be married to in the  resurrection well Jesus now what are you  gonna do an in typical Jesus fashion he  answers the question in a way that  points out the failings of the Sadducees  and also upholds the law essentially he  says marriages of this world and the  resurrection is totally different  there's no marrying them so it's not an  issue he takes on the content the  surface level the question and answers  it and then he continues and uses Moses  himself to support the idea of the  resurrection Moses says that God speaks  of being the God of Abraham the god of  Isaac the God of Jacob and God is the  god of the living not the dead some of  them get it and acknowledge that Jesus  has spoken well and all of them learn  their lesson and don't question him  again he puts them in their place and it  is a good story it shows us Jesus great  ability to not get tripped up by the  trick and then in the midst of the  rhetoric there really is a deep truth  about what happens when we die a deeper  truth is embedded in what appears to be  the surface level of the content what's  simply the vehicle for the trick there  is a deeper truth about life and death  and everlasting life and that brings me  to why it is that I haven't been able to  just set aside this text and focus on  one of the other texts some of you I  have known for many years and some of  you met my parents along the way as they  attended my ordination here as they came  for our wedding and later for the births  of Connor and Dylan were just to visit  and some of you knew that my mother had  Alzheimer's disease for many years for  more than five years perhaps even before  we left New Haven for Baltimore mom  didn't know us certainly not by name  sometimes not at all and for the last  three years or so she hasn't spoken  coherent sentences or even words we  didn't tell her when my father died  because we knew she wouldn't be able to  understand and we couldn't bear to tell  her over and over again this September  my mother had a stroke and she died in  mid-october not quite a month ago my  sister and brother and I were able to be  with her  with each other a lot during that last  month and I so experienced God's grace  as I found myself longing to be with her  as I knew the time together would was  drawing to a close it was a good month  for us and she died surrounded by our  laughter and our stories and our love  and Connor and Dylan have each in their  own ways helped me in these days and  weeks Connor who's 10 has cried with me  acknowledging the depth of my pain Dylan  has hugged me and held me when I cried  and then came a day two weeks ago at two  weeks after her death a day that also  was my father's birthday and because I  wasn't smart enough to notice that was  happening at the same time it was a hard  day and when I talked to me me and the  boys about it Dylan said you know where  nana is right now she's in heaven in the  kitchen making something special for  grand daddy's birthday he's so sure so  concrete he actually pictures both his  grandmother's Nana whom he knew and  grandma Jack who died before he was born  talking and cooking together he even  looks forward to when he's going to be  with them because he's sure he is  certain and he is literal he's eight  Dylan's literalness might appeal to the  Sadducees you can hear them jump on  board yeah well bad wife of that man be  cooking for her husband oh wait which  husband the second the fifth the seventh  and yet the Sadducees are so different  from Dylan because they use the literal  understanding of the Resurrection to try  and trick Jesus like so many of us they  see the logical obvious flaws and the  reasoning if there's a resurrection  whose wife's but will she be or how will  a child who has died recognized a parent  who dies 50 years later or how can there  be room for everybody how will people  find each other how will I find all the  people I know who don't know each other  and how  can we all be together if we don't know  each other and on and on if how what  about whose wife will she be anyway the  problems are obvious right the  shortfalls in thinking of heaven of  eternal life of the resurrection they're  obvious but for a child the problems  aren't obvious they don't matter many  children can just suspend disbelief and  see a truth that we mature ones are too  smart to notice the truth that Dylan  knows about life after death is a  certainty that those we love still are  he knows a truth that his nana is no  longer bound by Alzheimer's disease he  knows Ruth that her love for her husband  of more than 50 years still lives the  details may be literal and concrete as  befits an eight-year-old but the  underlying truth of love and wholeness  rings through Jesus held children before  his followers reminding them to be like  the child not to be literal but to be  open to be able to see truth beyond the  literal Jesus might say to Dylan you're  right nana's now able to love granddaddy  again Paul wrote to the Corinthians that  when he was a child he thought like a  child he spoke like a child and when he  became a man he thought like a man  perhaps the combination is what we  should seek for ourselves the ability to  be as open and true as a child while  freed from the literal details as adults  I'd like to finish with Madeleine  L'Engle again who along with my mother  taught me the most about life and death  about grief and about resurrection I  want to share some words taken from a  1990 interview reflecting on her first  experience with the death of someone  beloved her uncle and this will sound  familiar to some of you who have read  those early books about Meg and Calvin  and Charles Wallace I lost his name so  Madeleine L'Engle didn't feel she could  could ask her parents in the midst of  their own grief to talk about it and  explain it to her and here's what she  said so at age eight i postulated a  planet on which no one had any eyes of  course they would compensate perfectly  well but if one of us came from our  planet to that planet where no one had  any eyes and tried to describe a sunset  or a daffodil blooming through the snow  or a human face with a loving smile it  could not be done so age eight Madeleine  continues i postulated that when we die  we get something just as magnificent as  sight but just as no one from our planet  could describe site to someone on that  sightless planet so no one can describe  for us the wonders that God has waiting  for us when we die she continues I still  think that's pretty good theology we're  all good theologians when were eight we  tend to go downhill rapidly after that  there are words from both our sons at  ages eight and ten good theologians that  are truly the words of God written on my  heart the words from Dylan that evoke a  picture of Nana freed from Alzheimer's  loving my father the words of comfort  from Connor acknowledging how hard it is  for me to have lost both parents there  are words of hymns I love to tell the  story we each have our own words  on our hearts God's words come and many  different voices and indeed when they  are written on our hearts when our  hearts break God's words do fall in and  we can know even in the broken hearted  pneus of grief we can know with an  eight-year-old certainty that love lives  that wholeness is restored that there is  resurrection and life I know that my  Redeemer liveth thanks be to God amen
 

